---
// A schematic screenshot of what layout.jsx (in CodePanel above it)
// produces: workspace buttons, two terminals and a browser window tiled
// edge to edge — proof a tiled workspace isn't just one terminal. It
// depicts a screen — it is not an interface, so it carries no focusable
// content and is hidden from assistive tech entirely; the code that
// produces it is what's readable.
//
// None of this markup is tauler's own output — real tauler rasterizes a
// layout file itself, it doesn't build a DOM. Every pane below is
// hand-authored to look like what layout.jsx would produce, not rendered
// by anything that ran it. The one real tauler code in this demo is
// DemoColumn's own script: a `defineUnit` Unit (tauler-web/js/units.js,
// ADR 0037) decides *which* example is active. That Unit doesn't touch
// this file — it toggles `data-demo-frame`/`hidden` on markup this
// component already rendered.
//
// [data-screen] itself has no border, no padding and no gap between its
// bar row and the tiled area below — a screenshot has none of those
// either. A monitor bezel was tempting to draw here once, but this mock
// isn't framing a photo of a monitor, it's standing in for the pixels a
// screen actually shows; every inset here would be exactly the kind of
// empty reserved space this mock has already ruled out elsewhere (see
// the [data-gap-root] comment below).
//
// bg-black on [data-screen] is a backstop, not a design choice: every
// pane already paints its own background, so this should never actually
// show through. It exists so a future pane that forgets one fails
// obviously (a black hole) instead of leaking whatever's behind this
// component on the real page.
//
// The workspace-button strip up top isn't a simulated i3bar — it's what
// this whole page is arguing for: tauler replaces i3bar/polybar, so that
// strip is drawn exactly like the three cycling examples below it would
// draw one, not a separate "here's what a status bar looks like" bit.
//
// Every tiled pane below it is `border`, nothing else — no title bar, no
// on-screen "FOCUSED" label, no highlighted pane. Checked against a real
// i3 config's own `for_window [all] border pixel 1`: focus there is a
// border color, but this mock skips even that — which window has input
// isn't the story being told here, so nothing claims one does.
//
// The terminal's last two lines track CodePanel's cycling states
// (data-demo-frame, driven by DemoColumn's script) so the reload log
// always matches whichever example is on screen.
//
// There used to be a second indicator here too — a thin accent strip at
// whichever edge the active example's `anchor` declared, reserving space
// there even for examples this screen shows no other trace of. That's
// exactly the i3-gaps move this mock isn't making: empty space held open
// for its own sake. Reserved space only happens for the workspace bar
// below, because that one shows something real (workspace numbers, a
// clock) — not because an example merely *has* an anchor.
//
// Secondary text here is ink-3, not the usual ink-4: at this scale (10.5–
// 11.5px, well under the "large text" AA threshold) ink-4 on surface-frame
// measures ~4.46:1 — just under WCAG AA's 4.5:1 floor for small text.

interface Props {
  states: { id: string; reload: string; anchor: 'top' | 'right' | 'left' }[]
}

const { states } = Astro.props

// A real 1920×1080 page rendered at this pane's true size would be a few
// hundred px wide — its text would be a couple of pixels tall, not a
// legible sentence. Greeked bars, not real headlines: this is a
// screenshot, not a webpage you're meant to read. Widths are fixed (not
// random) so the build — and its visual-regression baseline — stays
// deterministic. The same goes for the URL bar: if the page's own text
// doesn't get to be readable, the address bar doesn't either — it's one
// more greeked bar, not a smaller font.
const feedRows = [
  { title: 82, meta: 32 },
  { title: 64, meta: 44 },
  { title: 90, meta: 28 },
  { title: 58, meta: 50 },
  { title: 75, meta: 34 },
  { title: 68, meta: 40 },
]

const tabs = [
  { active: false, title: 26 },
  { active: true, title: 30 },
  { active: false, title: 22 },
]

// i3bar's own workspace buttons (see draw_button/draw_bars in i3bar's
// xcb.c): a bordered box per workspace, three states — focused, active
// (visible on another output but not focused), inactive. One output only
// here, so "active" never applies.
const workspaces = [
  { number: 1, focused: true },
  { number: 2, focused: false },
  { number: 3, focused: false },
]

// The second terminal has nothing to say — it exists to prove tiling isn't
// one lucky terminal, the same job the browser's greeked feed rows already
// do. Widths fixed, not random, for the same determinism reason as
// feedRows above.
const fillerLines = [72, 45, 88, 30, 60, 50]
---

<div
  aria-hidden="true"
  data-screen
  data-active-anchor={states[0]?.anchor}
  class="flex flex-col bg-black font-ui shadow-panel"
>
  {
    /* The workspace/clock bar is a tauler panel too — the whole page's
       argument is that tauler renders bars, not just this one demo's
       three examples — so it isn't fixed chrome sitting above the cycle;
       it only shows when the active example actually declares a
       top-anchored panel (`load`, the only `anchor="top"` state). The
       other examples declare right/left panels instead, so there's
       nothing here for tauler to have rendered, and this row collapses
       entirely — see the <style> block for why that's `display`, not
       `visibility`. Driven by [data-screen]'s data-active-anchor, set by
       DemoColumn's script from whichever example is currently shown. */
  }
  <div
    data-top-bar
    class="flex items-center gap-[3px] border-b border-line-1 bg-surface-frame px-s2 py-1"
  >
    {
      workspaces.map((ws) => (
        <span
          class:list={[
            'border px-[4px] py-[1px] text-label-sm tracking-chrome',
            ws.focused
              ? 'border-accent bg-accent text-accent-ink'
              : 'border-line-2 text-ink-3',
          ]}
        >
          {ws.number}
        </span>
      ))
    }
    <span class="ml-auto text-label-sm tracking-chrome text-ink-3" data-clock
      >--:--</span
    >
  </div>
  <div data-gap-root class="relative">
    {
      /* No title bars anywhere below — checked against a real i3 config's
         `for_window [all] border pixel 1`: every window is *just* a
         border, no decoration, and nothing wraps the tiled area itself —
         a border there wouldn't belong to any window, it'd be a container
         chrome i3 has no equivalent of. So every pane below draws its own
         full border independently, the way a real i3 window does, on
         every side including the ones flush against another pane: two
         windows sitting edge to edge (no `gaps inner`/`outer` — that real
         i3 config leaves both commented out) each still draw their own
         border, so a shared seam is two 1px lines, not one. That's a
         thicker line at the seam, not a gap — nothing opens up between
         the panes, which is the distinction that matters (see the
         [data-gap-root] comment below for the actual thing this mock
         doesn't simulate). Every pane gets the same muted border — this
         mock isn't claiming to know which one has focus, only that a
         tiled screen holds more than one window at once. */
    }
    <div class="grid grid-cols-[2.6fr_1.4fr]">
      {
        /* Stacked top/bottom, not side by side — a horizontal split, one
           terminal above the other, sharing this column's width rather
           than each getting half of it. This wrapper is layout only, no
           border of its own — the two i3 windows inside it are what draw
           borders, not the column they happen to share. */
      }
      <div class="flex flex-col">
        <div class="flex flex-1 flex-col border border-line-2 bg-surface-frame">
          <div
            class="flex flex-col gap-s2 px-s3 py-s3 text-chrome-sm text-ink-2"
          >
            <p><span class="text-accent">❯</span> cargo run --release</p>
            <p class="text-ink-3">Compiling tauler</p>
            <p class="text-ink-3">Finished [optimized]</p>
            {
              states.map((s, i) => (
                <div data-demo-frame={s.id} hidden={i !== 0}>
                  <p>
                    <span class="text-accent">❯</span> tauler reload
                  </p>
                  <p class="text-ink-3">{s.reload}</p>
                </div>
              ))
            }
            <p><span class="text-accent">❯</span> _</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="flex flex-1 flex-col border border-line-2 bg-surface-frame">
          <div class="flex flex-col gap-s2 px-s3 py-s3">
            {
              fillerLines.map((w) => (
                <div class="h-[2px] bg-ink-4/40" style={`width:${w}%`} />
              ))
            }
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="flex flex-col border border-line-2 bg-surface-frame">
        <div class="flex items-end gap-px bg-line-1 px-[3px] pt-[3px]">
          {
            tabs.map((tab) => (
              <div
                class:list={[
                  'flex items-center gap-[3px] border-x border-t border-line-2 px-[5px] py-[3px]',
                  tab.active ? 'bg-surface-control' : 'bg-surface-frame',
                ]}
              >
                <span
                  class:list={[
                    'h-[4px] w-[4px] shrink-0',
                    tab.active ? 'bg-accent' : 'bg-ink-4/60',
                  ]}
                />
                <div
                  class:list={[
                    'h-[2px]',
                    tab.active ? 'bg-ink-2/55' : 'bg-ink-4/40',
                  ]}
                  style={`width:${tab.title}px`}
                />
                {tab.active && <span class="text-[7px] text-ink-4">×</span>}
              </div>
            ))
          }
          <span class="pb-[3px] pl-[3px] text-[9px] text-ink-4">+</span>
        </div>
        <div
          class="flex items-center gap-s2 border-b border-line-1 bg-surface-control px-s2 py-1"
        >
          <span class="text-[8px] text-ink-3">←</span>
          <span class="text-[8px] text-ink-4/60">→</span>
          <span class="text-[8px] text-ink-3">↻</span>
          <div
            class="flex min-w-0 flex-1 items-center border border-line-2 bg-surface-frame px-s2 py-[2px]"
          >
            <div class="h-[2px] w-24 bg-ink-3/40"></div>
          </div>
          <span class="text-[9px] text-ink-3">⋮</span>
        </div>
        {
          /* Real Hacker News has no dark mode — it's always its own cream
           page under whatever chrome is around it. The rest of this mock
           is our dark UI; this one surface deliberately isn't, because
           that's what the actual site looks like, tiled or not.

           flex-1: neither the tab strip nor the toolbar above it grow, so
           without this the cream page would stop at its own content height
           and whatever's left of the pane (16:9 leaves more room than this
           mock's content needs — see [data-screen]'s own comment) would
           show the pane's background instead — a dark stripe under a
           browser window, not more of the page. */
        }
        <div class="flex flex-1 flex-col bg-[#f6f6ef]">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-s2 bg-accent px-s2 py-[3px]">
            <span
              class="font-display text-[10px] font-semibold text-accent-ink"
            >
              Y
            </span>
            <div class="h-[3px] w-10 bg-accent-ink/45"></div>
            <div class="ml-auto h-[2px] w-14 bg-accent-ink/30"></div>
          </div>
          <div class="flex flex-col">
            {
              feedRows.map((row) => (
                <div class="flex items-center gap-s2 border-b border-black/10 px-s2 py-[5px] tight:py-1">
                  <span class="shrink-0 text-[8px] text-black/45">▲</span>
                  <div class="flex min-w-0 flex-1 flex-col gap-[3px]">
                    <div
                      class="h-[3px] bg-black/55"
                      style={`width:${row.title}%`}
                    />
                    <div
                      class="h-[2px] bg-black/30"
                      style={`width:${row.meta}%`}
                    />
                  </div>
                </div>
              ))
            }
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<script>
  // The visitor's own real clock — the one thing in the mock that is
  // genuinely live, not a picture of liveness. See CLAUDE.md / the demo
  // handoff: everything else in this schematic is static on purpose.
  function tick(): void {
    const now = new Date()
    const text =
      String(now.getHours()).padStart(2, '0') +
      ':' +
      String(now.getMinutes()).padStart(2, '0')
    for (const el of document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-clock]')) {
      el.textContent = text
    }
  }
  tick()
  setInterval(tick, 1000)
</script>

<style>
  /* [data-gap-root] no longer reserves anything of its own — no strip
     lives here any more (see the markup comment above the grid), so there
     is nothing left to make room for. It still needs to fill whatever
     column space [data-screen] has left over once the workspace bar
     (below) collapses, rather than leaving a gap where the bar used to
     be. */
  [data-gap-root] {
    display: flex;
    flex: 1;
    flex-direction: column;
  }

  /* The workspace/clock bar only has anything to show for the top-anchored
     example — see the markup comment above [data-top-bar]. `display`, not
     `visibility`: reserving its row's height for an example that never
     declared a bar there is exactly the empty-gap problem this mock
     doesn't simulate elsewhere either; the row actually collapses, and
     the flex-1/stretch chain below ([data-gap-root], then its grid) is
     what lets the tiled area grow to fill the space that opens up instead
     of leaving it blank. */
  [data-screen]:not([data-active-anchor='top']) [data-top-bar] {
    display: none;
  }

  /* A real screenshot has fixed pixel dimensions — a monitor doesn't
     resize itself. [data-screen] has no border or padding of its own (see
     the markup comment up top), so box-sizing: border-box isn't currently
     absorbing anything — it's here so a future border/padding addition
     stays compatible with the fixed size below instead of silently
     growing it. That fixed size is what makes the workspace bar's
     collapse free space inside the box rather than shrinking the box
     itself, so cycling through examples changes what's inside the screen,
     never the screen's own size. 560×315 is 16:9 — a common basic
     laptop's aspect ratio, and the same ratio as the 1920×1080 this mock
     is standing in for — not this content's own natural rendered height
     (which is shorter): the flex-1 stretch chain below, plus the browser
     pane's own flex-1 fill (see the markup comment on its white HN box),
     is what makes the extra height read as "more room in a real window"
     instead of dead space. */
  [data-screen] {
    box-sizing: border-box;
    width: 560px;
    height: 315px;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
  }

  /* Same 16:9, scaled down — not a different ratio for cramped viewports.
     432×243 is this mock's pre-16:9-fix size (560×243), which fit the
     laptop breakpoint's vertical budget; pairing 243 with a narrower 432
     width instead of 560 is what makes that height 16:9 again rather than
     a one-off exception. Threshold matches global.css's `tight` custom
     variant (max-width: 720px or max-height: 860px) — plain media query
     because `tight:` is a Tailwind class variant, not something a raw CSS
     value in a scoped <style> block can use. */
  @media ((max-width: 720px) or (max-height: 860px)) {
    [data-screen] {
      width: 432px;
      height: 243px;
    }
  }

  /* The other half of the stretch chain above: [data-gap-root]'s own
     single grid row (the tiled panes) fills whatever height it was given
     — CSS Grid's default stretch alignment for an auto-sized track with
     room to spare, not a fixed pane height in disguise. */
  [data-gap-root] > .grid {
    flex: 1;
  }
</style>
